{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1795295570,"nonce":"n5nCLX0bL8ugoO5aN6wL4+vMFgc=","message":"Step 1 is done: free of the last small object a thread allocated gives its bytes back to the thread's region.\n\nResult on the x86 NUC, release builds, Immix before and after, with the pool as reference. Python any_field, the row that motivated it:\n\n| | wall s | peak MB |\n|---|---|---|\n| pool | 0.019 to 0.035 | 23 to 28 |\n| Immix before | 0.036 to 0.045 | 58 to 61 |\n| Immix after | 0.023 to 0.030 | 23 to 25 |\n\nPeak memory now equals the pool's. That means each freed block was the last one at its free.\n\nThe other 42 rows are unchanged. A busy first pass moved 10 rows. A quiet rerun of those rows gave after over before between 0.93 and 1.08. So the first pass was noise from another project on the box. Outputs equal the pool's on every row.\n\nThe remaining gap on py free_function_call, free_function_call_untyped and inlined_call is about 10 ms on a 30 ms run. It exists before this change too. Each allocation and free goes through two calls into the heap table, and the pool does neither. That is the inline-bump item already listed on this issue.\n\nCorrectness. Unit tests cover the last block, a block under another, a repeated free, an interior address and a free with no open region. Lua conformance under Immix passes, including official and warm_up::official at the default floor. One run with ZYNTAX_IMMIX_FLOOR_KB=256 failed official/cstack.lua with a signal, not a wrong answer. At that floor a collection runs every 256 KB of allocation. Each one scans the deep stack. The file took 127 s and 3.2 GB instead of 1.2 s and 111 MB. The default floor is fine. This is the same pacing weakness as 3bbb229: collection cost follows stack depth, and a fixed floor ignores it.\n\nNot done here: medium objects (129 B to 16 KB) are not rolled back. No benchmark row needs it yet.\n\nLanded in 6c567fe2.","files":null}]}